  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong as a sparse, evolvable wire substrate: deterministic CBOR, exact-byte opaque forwarding, explicit unsupported-pCID failure, and wrapper-selected signatures preserve clear layer boundaries without a central registry. Weak as direct evidence for this scenario because it does not define multi-hop discovery, route selection, or acceptable-path policy; those remain in higher-layer payload protocols.",
    "strengths": [
      "Unknown-pCID store/forward and opaque payload handling fit sparse-knowledge and sparse-CAS assumptions.",
      "Deterministic CBOR and exact-byte preservation support later byte-level audit.",
      "Wrapper-by-pCID signatures keep integrity/authorship extensible without changing the base envelope."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No routing advertisement, path-policy, or neighbor-discovery semantics are specified.",
      "Auditability of meaning depends on extra payload and wrapper protocols, not the base envelope alone.",
      "The specimen is still draft and unfrozen, so long-horizon interoperability evidence is provisional."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Peers may preserve unknown routing envelopes but still be unable to evaluate acceptable paths without shared handlers.",
      "Nested wrapper schemes could make multi-hop routing evidence harder to validate and compare."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
